WEBVTT

00:03.840 --> 00:05.320
Okay, so what is the count function?

00:05.320 --> 00:06.280
It's really simple.

00:06.280 --> 00:09.440
It can return the number of rows within each window.

00:09.440 --> 00:14.200
So it can help you to understand how many rows do you have within each subset of data.

00:14.200 --> 00:17.480
So now let's go and understand how SQL works with this function.

00:17.480 --> 00:18.040
All right guys.

00:18.040 --> 00:22.240
So now we have again this very simple example where the orders and we have the following informations.

00:22.240 --> 00:23.880
We have the products and sales.

00:24.200 --> 00:26.440
And now we want to solve very simple task.

00:26.440 --> 00:29.360
How many orders do we have within each products.

00:29.520 --> 00:33.080
So in order to solve it we can use the function count like the following.

00:33.200 --> 00:38.440
So we can say count and then we pass for it an argument or expression the star.

00:38.440 --> 00:40.880
So with that we are telling SQL go and count.

00:40.920 --> 00:43.160
How many rows do we have in our table.

00:43.160 --> 00:47.520
But we have a window definition like this over partition by products.

00:47.520 --> 00:48.560
So now what is SQL going to do?

00:48.560 --> 00:51.720
I'm going to go and divide the data sets into two partitions.

00:51.720 --> 00:56.000
We're going to have one partition for the caps and another one for the gloves.

00:56.000 --> 00:58.480
So with that SQL prepared our data into windows.

00:58.480 --> 01:00.350
And we are ready to do aggregations.

01:00.350 --> 01:02.310
So how many rows do we have within each window?

01:02.350 --> 01:03.230
It's going to be three.

01:03.230 --> 01:05.510
So for this window it's going to be three rows.

01:05.990 --> 01:08.910
And as well for the next window we have as well three rows.

01:08.950 --> 01:11.350
So we can have three three and three.

01:11.390 --> 01:12.790
It's very simple right guys.

01:12.790 --> 01:15.870
We are just finding the number of rows within each window.

01:15.870 --> 01:21.870
But now with the aggregate functions we have to be very careful with the null values for the count star.

01:21.870 --> 01:25.350
As you can see over here, we are not specifying anything about the sales.

01:25.350 --> 01:27.590
So we are just saying find me the number of rows.

01:27.590 --> 01:31.270
So that means SQL will just count the nulls as one row.

01:31.270 --> 01:36.390
So that means if we are using the star as an argument for the function counts, the null will not affect

01:36.390 --> 01:36.870
anything.

01:36.870 --> 01:42.030
So whether we have nulls or not, we are just counting how many rows do we have inside our data.

01:42.030 --> 01:45.950
But in some scenarios we should be ignoring the nulls in our count.

01:45.990 --> 01:51.150
For example, let's say that I would like to count how many sales do we have within each product.

01:51.310 --> 01:54.510
That means if we have nulls, it should not be counted.

01:54.750 --> 01:59.670
So now in order to achieve this task, what we're going to do, we're going to use instead of star.

01:59.710 --> 02:02.350
Over here we're going to have the field sales.

02:02.350 --> 02:05.670
So now with this we are telling SQL don't just count blindly.

02:05.670 --> 02:07.550
How many rows do we have within each window?

02:07.870 --> 02:09.950
You should be very careful with the values.

02:10.150 --> 02:13.390
Find how many sales do we have within each window.

02:13.550 --> 02:16.270
So now let's see what's going to happen for the first window.

02:16.310 --> 02:17.470
We have three sales.

02:17.470 --> 02:18.910
So we have three values.

02:18.910 --> 02:20.670
So the number of rows is correct.

02:20.670 --> 02:23.030
But for the next one how many sales do we have.

02:23.070 --> 02:24.230
We have two.

02:24.390 --> 02:26.910
So we have this sale and then the 70.

02:26.950 --> 02:28.190
But the last one is null.

02:28.190 --> 02:29.870
So it will not be counted.

02:29.990 --> 02:31.070
It will be ignored.

02:31.310 --> 02:34.270
That's why we're going to get in the output the value two.

02:34.310 --> 02:35.470
We have two sales.

02:35.470 --> 02:37.550
So as you can see the result did change.

02:37.550 --> 02:40.270
And we are now more sensitive to the null values.

02:40.270 --> 02:42.870
So be careful what you are specifying for the count.

02:42.870 --> 02:46.270
If you are using a column name like this, it will ignore the nulls.

02:46.470 --> 02:51.790
But if you have a star, it's just going to go and find how many rows do we have within each partition.

02:51.830 --> 02:56.470
Okay, so now if you go and compare the results side by side, you can see that if you specify a column

02:56.630 --> 02:58.220
within the count function.

02:58.220 --> 03:02.860
It's going to be sensitive with the nulls, so it's going to ignore it and will not use it within the

03:02.860 --> 03:03.500
aggregations.

03:03.500 --> 03:05.580
That's why we have here only two rows.

03:05.820 --> 03:09.820
But if you go and use the star within the count function, what's going to happen?

03:09.860 --> 03:11.580
SQL is just going to go and count it.

03:11.580 --> 03:15.060
So we're going to find the number of rows that we have inside our table.

03:15.060 --> 03:19.860
And there is one more way in order to do the same thing here on the left side you can use instead of

03:19.860 --> 03:21.540
star, you can use a one.

03:21.740 --> 03:24.140
So you might find it somewhere that people are using.

03:24.180 --> 03:27.060
Count one and then the same with the function.

03:27.060 --> 03:29.060
And we will get exactly the same result.

03:29.060 --> 03:32.060
So the nulls will be counted and will not be ignored.

03:32.060 --> 03:35.060
So now you might ask me which one should I use the one or the star?

03:35.100 --> 03:37.260
Well I would say it doesn't matter, right?

03:37.260 --> 03:42.180
We are getting the same results and if you are thinking about the performance, I hardly find any differences

03:42.180 --> 03:42.940
between them.

03:42.940 --> 03:48.420
So you can go and try both of them and stick with the one that is giving you like more better performance.

03:48.420 --> 03:52.420
Now we have special case for the count function compared to all other aggregate functions.

03:52.660 --> 03:54.420
It allows any data type.

03:54.420 --> 03:56.140
So that means we can use numbers.

03:56.140 --> 03:58.330
We can use characters, dates and so on.

03:58.330 --> 04:02.410
So that means we can go and specify something like the products for the count instead of sales.

04:02.410 --> 04:05.490
So we can go over here and say products, and it's going to go and count.

